      "id": 159137,
      "name": "TheCraftyCurler",
      "username": "TheCraftyCurler",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/t/d6d6ee/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2016-02-10T06:08:18.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"VisforYoshi\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/visforyoshi/48/2185_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> VisforYoshi:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Well, this isn't me asking for an errata, but this is the most approriate thread to bring it up in.Does anybody else get bugged by the art in \"Gone without a trace\" from Madame Mittermier's? Argent Adept didnt leave without a trace, Tempest found his pipes. This probably bugs me more than it should, but am I the only one that it annoys?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I caught that, too; however, it's entirely incumbent on what you define as \"a trace.\" You could easily argue that, yes, finding the pipes IS a trace of evidence left by Argent Adept. Personally, I viewed it as, \"there is no trace, or path, for them to follow to find him.\" You know he's missing by the pipes on the ground, but that's it. No other clues, hints, or evidence exists to allow you the means of finding him.<br><br>Then again, that could just be my rationalization of the situation to have it all make sense. &nbsp;</p>",
